WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.060
Some pairs of heroes have decades-old rivalries
so heated it's almost a letdown when they

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.080
work together rather than fight each other.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.280
But those rivalries aren't always based on
meaningful differences.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.420
If you don't believe us, check out these superhero
rivalries that don't make any sense.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.660
Batman vs. Superman

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.420
Frank Miller’s classic Dark Knight Returns
is one of the most influential Batman stories

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:26.130
of all time, and one of its most memorable
moments comes at the climax, when Batman takes

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.700
on Superman in what seems like a fight to
the death.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:29.880
Sound familiar?

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.680
"What does that mean?"

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.060
Of course, Superman had been hit with a literal
nuclear explosion and a tank before Batman

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:42.930
threw his first punch, and a Kryptonite Arrow
from Ollie Queen during the fight, but a win

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:43.500
was a win.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:49.000
This was the moment that took the aging Bruce
Wayne from a fallible human to a master strategist

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.120
who could take anyone down.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:55.060
Before this story, no one really imagined
that Batman could present a physical challenge

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:59.780
to Superman, but with the success of a single
story, it became their default relationship.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:04.020
Unfortunately, if you think about it for about
two seconds, it makes no sense.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.820
If they really took each other on, Superman
could blast Batman with heat vision from orbit

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.700
before the Caped Crusader could hop off whatever
gargoyle he was perched on.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.880
Then he could freeze the ashes with super-breath,
and throw it all into the sun.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:22.200
The idea that Batman is cool enough to beat
anyone with enough prep time actually ignores

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.540
what’s really cool about Batman in the first
place.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:28.320
If he’s the guy who’s smart enough to
figure out how to beat Superman, then he should

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.979
probably be smart enough to figure out that
he could get a lot more crime-fighting done

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.760
if a flying, bulletproof, super-strong farm
boy was on his side.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:38.280
Daredevil vs. Punisher

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.960
Daredevil and Punisher have a rollercoaster
relationship where they find themselves teaming

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.900
up as allies just as much as they take each
other on as enemies.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:52.159
When they work together, even after all these
years, Daredevil somehow seems surprised when

00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:56.920
the guy with the skull on his chest who carries
all the military grade weaponry tries to kill

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.460
someone, and that's when the honeymoon ends.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.950
The thing is, either one of these two could
easily end their rivalry without doing all

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:04.500
that much.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.760
On one hand, Daredevil doesn’t have much
of a leg to stand on here.

00:02:08.769 --> 00:02:13.319
He’s ostensibly battling the Punisher because
of his tendency to execute criminals without

00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:17.620
giving them a fair trial first, but if Matt
Murdock really cared about the letter of the

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.140
law, he probably wouldn’t spend his nights
dressed up as the devil punching out ninjas.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:27.200
Given that Frank Castle is usually portrayed
as pretty meticulous in selecting his targets,

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.760
maybe just take the lighter workload as a
win and leave him to it.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:34.640
On the other hand, if he absolutely has to
murder criminals, Punisher might consider

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.600
just doing it somewhere other than Hell's
Kitchen.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:37.960
Seriously, Frank.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.400
Daredevil doesn’t really leave Manhattan
that often.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.700
Just go kill people in Brooklyn and you’ll
be fine.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:44.900
Flash vs. Green Lantern

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:50.559
When Kyle Rayner replaced Hal Jordan as Green
Lantern in the '90s, it was more than a little

00:02:50.559 --> 00:02:55.189
controversial, with a vocal group of fans
demanding the return of the previous Green

00:02:55.189 --> 00:02:55.680
Lantern.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.620
They weren’t the only ones that were looking
down on Kyle, though.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.980
They had an ally in one of his own teammates:
Wally West, better known as the Flash.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:10.540
When the Justice League's flagship title was
relaunched in 1997 as JLA, Rayner and West

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.260
found themselves on the same team.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.480
And while other members of the League had
doubts about the new Green Lantern, Wally

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.840
West was a straight-up jerk to him for most
of their time together.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.060
Flash constantly trashed the new Lantern from
the very first issue, often suggesting or

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.560
outright saying he wasn't worthy of his power
ring.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.800
Where did Wally's rage come from?

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.959
Wally had been a superhero since he was a
child as Kid Flash, and was upholding a legacy

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.100
that had been passed down to him by his mentor.

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:41.840
Kyle, on the other hand, was literally just
handed the most powerful weapon in the universe

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.100
at random out of desperation without ever
even paying his dues as a sidekick.

00:03:46.309 --> 00:03:50.860
He was, however, the only Green Lantern at
the time, so what exactly did the Flash think

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.740
he was going to accomplish with his insults?

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.100
It’s not like they could’ve gotten a replacement.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:58.020
Maybe he was just happy to not be the youngest
guy on the team anymore.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.580
The Thing vs. Man-Thing

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:06.440
The Man-Thing and Ben Grimm, better known
to Fantastic Four fans as the Thing, didn’t

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:10.660
exactly have the storied rivalry of some of
the other pairs on this list.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.520
Their conflict only lasted one issue, but
it’s worth mentioning for being one of the

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.800
most remarkably stupid reasons for two heroes
to fight that comics had ever seen.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.170
The first issue of Marvel Two-In-One, a series
where Grimm teamed up with other heroes from

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:27.980
throughout the universe, opens with Bashful
Benjamin reading a newspaper article.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.949
He becomes so furious that the swamp monster
has “stolen” the word “Thing” that

00:04:32.949 --> 00:04:37.300
he takes a bus to the Florida Everglades in
order to beat him up for copyright infringement.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:43.250
In typical comic book fashion, the two monstrous
good guys wind up teaming up to fight a supervillain

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:43.740
instead.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:48.080
In the end, the Thing and Man-Thing don't
really fight all that much.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.540
But just consider that in the first page of
the issue, the Thing is in New Mexico.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:58.560
Imagine taking a bus trip from New Mexico
to Florida as a giant, mostly naked monster

00:04:58.569 --> 00:05:02.900
so that you can beat up another monster for
having a name that’s kind of the same as

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:03.400
yours.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:09.120
If that’s what the FF considers worthy of
violence, then maybe Doctor Doom actually

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:09.860
is the good guy here.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.140
Superman vs. Shazam

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:17.500
Superman and Captain Marvel, better known
as Shazam, have had a rivalry that dates back

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:20.600
to the 1940s, and goes well beyond the comics.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:26.919
Back then, DC sued Shazam’s publisher, Fawcett
Publications, claiming that their World’s

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:29.900
Mightiest Mortal was nothing but a knockoff
of the Man of Steel.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:35.000
After winning the lawsuit in the ‘50s, DC
would license the Shazam characters and add

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:39.640
them to their universe where they moved their
fight back onto the page more often than not.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:44.419
It doesn’t take a hardcore comic book fan
to figure out why this particular rivalry

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:45.560
doesn't make any sense.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:50.220
It seems mostly based on two people beating
each other up because they have similar powers

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:51.620
and the shared love of capes.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:56.260
What’s really disturbing about it, though,
is that Shazam is literally a child.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:06:00.120
Billy Batson might transform into a body that
looks pretty grown-up, but he’s still a

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:00.800
kid at heart.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:05.980
That means that Superman, emphasis on the
“man,” has spent their entire rivalry

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:07.620
getting into fistfights with a boy.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:13.220
DC may have won the lawsuit, but in the panels,
it’s definitely not a good look for Superman.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:14.000
Think about it.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:16.500
When these two fight, if Superman wins?

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:18.380
Then Superman just beat up a kid.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:19.900
If Superman loses?

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.100
Then Superman just got beat up by a kid.

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:26.420
Superman should do everything he can to avoid
a fight with Captain Marvel.

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:29.300
Let him stay up past nine or play all the
Fortnite he wants.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:35.100
It’s worth it not to be known as the superhero
who beats up on, or gets beat up by, grade

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:35.600
schoolers.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:37.820
The Avengers vs. the Defenders

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:43.780
Since they first gathered together on a day
like no other in 1963, the Avengers have been

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:45.800
made up of Earth’s mightiest heroes.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.220
The Defenders, on the other hand... have not.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:53.160
Despite occasionally sharing members, including
the Hulk and Doctor Strange, the Defenders

00:06:53.169 --> 00:06:57.340
have always been a second-string team that
deals with the weirder side of the Marvel

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:57.920
Universe.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:02.500
In other words, they don’t have much of
a reason to interact at all, let alone throw

00:07:02.509 --> 00:07:04.800
down in a massive, multi-issue fight.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.620
That’s exactly what happened in 1973, when
Marvel launched a crossover event known as

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:12.900
the Avengers/Defenders war.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.920
Spread out over eight issues which would be
a long story today, let alone back in the

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:22.560
‘70s the event featured members of both
teams battling it out to capture pieces of

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:24.560
an artifact called the Evil Eye.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:29.200
It wouldn’t be the last time they’d fight,
but it was for the dumbest possible reason.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:35.040
The demonic Dormammu and Loki manipulated
the teams so that each one thought the others

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.600
were gathering up the Evil Eye’s pieces
for nefarious purposes.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:42.780
You’d think this could all be solved if
Captain America just called up Doctor Strange

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.979
and asked what was going on, but to be fair,
the Defenders’ roster boasted the Hulk and

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:50.480
Prince Namor, both of whom have occasionally
been supervillains.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:54.699
That said, the actual reason the Avengers
turned on their would-be compatriots came

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:56.920
when Loki you know, the God of Lies?

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:00.400
showed up at the mansion and told them the
Defenders were up to no good.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:05.740
Since they had absolutely no reason at all
to believe that the lying liar who hated them

00:08:05.759 --> 00:08:10.469
and wanted to destroy them might not be telling
the whole truth, the fight was on.

00:08:10.469 --> 00:08:11.840
Think it through next time, guys.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.120
Superman vs. Flash

00:08:14.629 --> 00:08:19.540
The ongoing contest between Superman and the
Flash isn’t the kind of violent conflict

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:23.439
where the characters come to blows, but it’s
gone on long enough to be one of the greatest

00:08:23.439 --> 00:08:25.729
rivalries in superhero history.

00:08:25.729 --> 00:08:32.110
Ever since 1967's Superman #199, the Flash
and the last son of Krypton have occasionally

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:37.500
raced because of the question fans have wanted
answered for decades Who's faster?

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:38.800
Superman or the Flash?

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:44.060
It's a good question, but with over 50 years
behind us, it’s a little weird that we’re

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:44.640
still asking it.

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:50.120
Since both characters count super-speed among
their powers their only power, in the Flash’s

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:54.120
case they’re both limited only by the creators’
imaginations.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:58.900
As a result, the arguments in favor of each
hero have gotten truly ridiculous.

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:04.220
The Flash is fast enough to vibrate through
solid objects, but Superman can’t!

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:10.120
Superman is fast enough to time travel under
his own power, but Flash needs a special high-tech

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:11.380
treadmill to do it!

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:12.520
It’s weird stuff.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.880
At the end of the day, though, Superman is
bulletproof, strong enough to bench-press

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:21.420
mountains, can jump to the moon without breaking
a sweat, and shoot lasers out of his eyes

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:22.710
that can blow up cars.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:27.740
Can’t he just let the guy with “The Fastest
Man Alive” on the cover of his comic win

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:29.020
this one once and for all?

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.080
Hulk vs. Wolverine

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:37.020
The absolute king of nonsensical superhero
rivalries also happens to be one of the most

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:39.930
popular — Wolverine and the Incredible Hulk.

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:44.390
There is something undeniably cool about the
match, and it really does fit with Wolverine's

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:45.390
character.

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:50.440
He’s a small but vicious warrior set against
the biggest, strongest monster he could find.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:55.010
It makes for good art and maybe even a good
issue – one good issue.

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:56.440
Maybe two.

00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:01.570
Wolverine is a scrappy brawler with unbreakable
bones and razor-sharp claws, but he’s also

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:06.130
the kind of hero who’s frequently beaten
up by another guy with claws who’s slightly

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:06.620
taller.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:12.220
The Hulk, on the other hand, has punched out
gods, lifted mountains on his back, and held

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:16.540
his own against every other hero in the Marvel
Universe on more than one occasion.

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:21.460
Wolverine isn’t the guy who’s really going
to go toe-to-toe with him and pick up a victory.

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:26.910
It’s even backed up by their very first
meeting in Incredible Hulk #181, when the

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:30.680
Hulk took Wolverine out with one relatively
light bonk on the head.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:34.040
Maybe stick to slugging it out with Cyclops
from now on, Logan.

